Hi guys,

Well, here it is, my 2nd assencion post, a good year or so after my
first one, but largely due to me playing slashem for most of that year
(Im still trying to asscend my lycanthrope in slashem... always did
like a challange).

Before I get into the (sketchy) detail of the assencion, I want to
thank whoever it was who released a patched version of nethack with
coloured menus, dumplog with extinct monsters, a 'different' inventory
order (still not sure if I like it or not), and other handy features
that should be in the official version (though the menu colour patch
was a bit buggy at times). It was because of him that I downloaded it
and thought 'I'll just try a random character to see the changes'.
64,000 turns later here I am, with my 2nd asencion, and I have to say,
after playing many many games of slashem with a lycanthrope, in
comparison this was a piece of cake!

Into the assencion itself, fairly run of the mill, have to say starting
with a fully armoured character with a handy (if not har to keep fed)
pet was very nice. Sure the knight conducts are a little anoying, but
not that hard to get used to keeping them (throw a non-weapon / tool at
any sleeping monster before you attack it. Throwing rocks even works,
even though against low level monsters more often than not it will kill
them).

I had a hairy moment in the mines where I dropped my BoH (yes, I was
lucky enough to find an early one) deep in the mines for a second to
unburden myself, then hit a teletrap and was cornered by 3 gnomes
without my bag of goddies... luckily I was carrying a wand of poly, so
when I got low on HP I zapped it at myself (had prayed recently), and
turned into a skeleton with full health. Not the monster I would have
chosen, but it was good enough to let me survive (their slow monster
attack is quite handy).

After that it was pretty straight forward, did mines (and got myself
excalibur), then soko (reflection), was going to do the castle before
the quest to get the wand and MR, but found a grey dragon on the way
there that was nice enough to leave me scales, so I made my own GDSM to
go with my AoR (although I personally prefer the SDSM / CoMR / AoLS
combo).

Not knowing what to expect from the quest, I was a little worried, so I
did most of it with a ring of conflict and ring of regeneration, but I
realised I had nothing to worry about. The filler levels were nothing
to worry about, with quasits being a pushover, and even the final level
with Ixoth was a piece of cake, me two-weponing excalibur and an elven
broadsword (not sure why I chose that for my other weapon, it was
probably the first thing to come along, but it did the job), and with
conflict, it took about 3 or 4 hits on him (and all the time he was
busy attacking something else). On the way back from there I spent a
bit of time on the alter in the quest and got snickersnee (boo) and
then Frost Brand (yay!), which then bacame my primary weapon (although
excalibur's auto searching is handy to have).

Prior to getting to the castle I had most of my AK, I had found GDS,
AoR, speed boots, and GoD (most was at +0 - +2 at that stage), but
again, being worried of anything going wrong, I used conflict again,
and again found it was a piece of cake (that ring can be a game
breaker... that along with +CM, which I found early on). I used one of
the wishes for ?oC, and on for the HoB, not sure what else... maybe a
/oD late in the game, and thats it.

Prior to going to hell I set up a camp on the level above the castle,
which happened to have an alter (only one in main dungeon), and polyed
a heap of my spellboks, rings, wands, armour, etc, and ended up with a
=oL (essential), +oI (very handy), and a bunch of other nice stuff, but
no /oD, AoLS or CoMR, which I was hanging out for! Still, enchanted
most of my stuff to +5 / +7 or thereabouts, and went into hell.

Again, not much to report, managed to charm a few purple worms, but
they always managed to get killed not by other monsters, but by my pet
titon whenever he got confused. Seriously, he killed 3 of them (with a
trident of all weapons, he we never interested in any other weapons,
though I never bothered giving him one of my artifact weapons, he'd
just lose it somewhere), and also one of my Umber Hulks (great pets to
have in the mazes to clear out paths).

Did a bit of alchemy before waking Yendor to get my max HP to just
under 600 (got lowered a bit in the end), knocked him off with Orcus's
/oD, got the amulet from the sanctum (again conflict was nice,
especially when rodney came in and double troubled, as soon as I put
conflict on he left me alone and went for the high priest). Got held
back a few times on the way back in hell, but once I got out of there
never again (is this always the case?), rodney appeared once or twice,
but whenever he did, he always double troubled, which was anoying, but
nothing a /oD cant fix.

The planes were nice (again, conflict for the tricky ones, but I need
not have bothered). I got attacked by a few air elements, but with AC
of -38 they weren't doing much damage (and I was!). The astral plane
wasnt too bad, took of my ring of conflict before I went in to get my
guardian angel, but then when I put if back on I was surprised that
that anoyed my guardian... there you go. No matter, with conflict it
was just a matter of finding the gaps to go through to get to the
alter, took me 3 visits, but was very smooth, accidently shot death
with a wand of death, before using MM to knock him out, the others was
with /oDeath. To be extra safe I replaced my AoR with AoLS (in case one
of the player characters insta killed me with an artifact weapon), but
never came close to needing it, the only artifact they were carrying
was a +7 giantslayer.

Thats it, here is my dump, comments welcome! (yes, the final dump is
all out of whack, blame Google (or maybe me!))

One question I have going over my (unsnipped) list of kills, it seems
to be in order of difficulty, but it some monsters seem was out of
order. For examply giant mimics are above rock / ice trolls, and large
mimics are above air elements! Also, fire/soldier ants are fairly low,
but they can be quite hard to hit, and pretty difficult. Is this just
ordered by the monster 'level' I take it?

